{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794425888,"nonce":"J/WJ2iPtRrhb8gYyjYIOkwsQQBk=","message":"Committed as 75e1b8a, not pushed.\n\nWindows goes through CI: .github/workflows/ci.yml. None of what follows has run on Windows; the first push of the workflow is the first run.\n\nWhat the workflow does:\n- `rust`: `cargo test --workspace` on ubuntu, windows and macos, with rustfmt and clippy (`-D warnings`) on ubuntu. On Windows the fixture's two HashLink cdylibs are excluded, since they would not link without libhl.lib.\n- `miri`: hxn-abi, and hxn-future less the four slice-of-time tests, with -Zmiri-ignore-leaks.\n- `hashlink`: tests/cli/run.sh hl and tests/examples/run.sh hl on ubuntu (HashLink 1.15 built with `make libhl hl`) and windows (the 1.15 release zip); tests/counter/run.sh hl and gen-hl on ubuntu only.\n- `hxcpp`: the same two scripts with cpp on ubuntu and windows (hxcpp 4.3.2), and counter cpp and gen-cpp on ubuntu.\n- The toolchain is pinned to nightly-2026-08-25, the one the lints were last clean on locally.\n\nWhat was changed for Windows, by reading and not by running:\n- The adapter crate's build script, for a Windows target, emits `rustc-link-lib=dylib=libhl` and a search path from HXN_HL_DIR, which the command sets to the VM's directory unless the environment has it.\n- An hxcpp adapter is built with `-C target-feature=+crt-static` on Windows, because hxcpp's MSVC toolchain compiles with -MT.\n- The generated build XML names kernel32, ntdll, userenv, ws2_32 and dbghelp for Windows, which is what `rustc --print native-static-libs` lists for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.\n- `hxn new --hxn` no longer canonicalizes the checkout, which on Windows gives a `\\\\?\\` path.\n- A VM named without `.exe` is found on PATH.\n- .gitattributes keeps LF on checkout; the tests compare text, and the readme test splits on a blank line.\n- The test scripts strip carriage returns from what a program prints.\n\nStill as the issue says: tests/counter/run.sh and the fixture's own adapter crates assume Unix names and have no Windows build, so the full fixture does not run there; `hxn_slice` by value on Win64 is unchecked beyond what the examples pass.\n\nAlso new and unrun: hxcpp on Linux, which CI now covers.","files":null}]}